{"custom_id": "81127d3828b2fea8152be8c946d4d553af5056e6ea73060a", "meta": {"loop": "topk4L02", "task": "infer_discrepancy_patterns_1", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "4d7dc6bee0c73352328c136e039c7cbf8717362ff8cd6f83d2e91c29f953f355", "prompt_chars": 10999}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_017vuPQBMXMbU5QJdLT6sNog", "saved_utc": "2026-10-23T10:36:10+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011CgK1jXQgPfpWCkBKTHXqr", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "I'm checking two missed disease mentions — \"unilateral retinal telangiectasis\" appearing alongside \"Coats' disease of the retina,\" and \"retinal telangiectasis\" itself referenced parenthetically as an alternate term.\n\n3. \"deficient activity of fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase\" is a biochemical deficiency phrase, likely tagged SpecificDisease because it's the molecular defining feature of SLS rather than a simple finding.\n4. \"glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency\" is a disease name with an embedded parenthetical abbreviation — the model probably mistook the enzyme name for a gene/protein mention and tagged it O instead of SpecificDisease.\n\nLooking at \"deficiency in G6PD,\" I see the same pattern: an enzyme deficiency phrase where G6PD reads as a gene name, so the model treats the whole span as non-disease. The common failure mode seems to be diseases expressed as compositional \"X deficiency/telangiectasis\" phrases built on an enzyme or anatomical noun rather than named with canonical disease-head words like \"syndrome\" or \"disease\" — the model latches onto the gene/finding head instead of recognizing the full phrase as the disease name. True positives, by contrast, tend to use canonical naming patterns.\n\nComparing against TPs, \"Coats telangiectasis\" is correctly matched because the eponym \"Coats\" anchors it as a disease name, unlike bare \"retinal telangiectasis,\" which lacks that proper-noun signal and instead reads as a descriptive gloss.\n\nThe pattern seems to be whether the mention's head noun is canonical disease nomenclature (disease, syndrome, an eponym/acronym) versus a descriptive phrase headed by a pathological sign or molecular defect term (like \"telangiectasis\" or \"deficiency\") combined with a gene/anatomical modifier. But cases like \"G6PD deficiency\" complicate this, since \"deficiency\" is often itself a disease head, and embedding a gene symbol inside the phrase seems to throw off the model's judgment.\n\nSame pattern with \"deficiency in G6PD\" - the disease-bearing word is \"deficiency\" while the model locks onto the gene name \"G6PD\" as the entity, tagging it O instead. So the general rule seems to be: when a disease name is built as \"[gene/enzyme/anatomy] + [deficiency/telangiectasis],\" the model attaches to the gene/anatomy token rather than the relational noun carrying the disease status, causing a miss. But eponym-anchored or syndrome/disease/acronym-headed names (\"Coats telangiectasis,\" \"Sjogren-Larsson syndrome,\" \"Norrie disease\") get tagged correctly because they carry an explicit proper-noun or disease-class anchor.\n\nLooking at the Coats' disease example more carefully though — the parenthetical \"unilateral retinal telangiectasis\" sits right next to \"Coats' disease\" yet is still gold-labeled separately as SpecificDisease, even without its own eponym or disease-class head inside the span itself, which complicates my proposed boolean anchor feature.\n\nTracing through the other examples, the pattern becomes clearer: discrepancies consistently involve a generic pathological-finding or biochemical-state head noun (telangiectasis, deficiency, deficient activity) paired with a gene/enzyme or anatomical modifier, often introduced via a metalinguistic naming cue like \"so-called\" or a parenthetical gloss — while the true positives like \"Coats telangiectasis\" share the same head noun but gain correct classification from an eponym anchor. This confirms the trigger explains every case, so my pattern insight should state: tag a span as SpecificDisease when a defect/finding-headed noun phrase with a gene/enzyme/anatomical modifier functions as a disease name (via naming markers or enzyme-deficiency constructions), without letting the embedded gene/enzyme symbol reclassify the span as a non-disease entity — I'll present this as one Markdown Pattern Insight with Pattern Name, Confusion Trigger, Contrastive Evidence, and Linguistic Rule sections, keeping the analysis concise before it.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "**Step 1: Deep Dive Analysis**\n\n- **Contrast:** The discrepancies and the true positives differ in whether the disease span contains a *nomenclature anchor*: an eponym (Coats, Norrie, Sjogren-Larsson), a disease-class head (disease, syndrome), or an established acronym (SLS). Every discrepancy span lacks one. Their disease-denoting head is a generic pathology or biochemical-state noun (telangiectasis, deficiency, deficient activity), and the modifier is an anatomical site or a gene/enzyme name.\n- **Isolate Trigger:** The model appears to read the head noun as a finding or measured state, and the embedded anatomical or gene/enzyme token as the entity. Neither reading maps to SpecificDisease, so the whole span falls to O. The text supports this: \"so-called retinal telangiectasis\" is a metalinguistic gloss that introduces a disease term, and \"deficiency in G6PD\" uses a gene symbol as the complement of a heritable-disorder head.\n- **Hypothesis Check:**\n  - Ex 1 and 2: \"telangiectasis\" with an anatomical modifier, introduced by a parenthetical or \"so-called\" gloss. Nomenclature anchor absent.\n  - Ex 3: \"deficient activity of [enzyme]\" with no anchor. The phrase defines SLS, so it is treated as the disease name even though it resembles a symptom list.\n  - Ex 4 and 5: \"deficiency\" with an enzyme/gene complement. Ex 4 also has a nested parenthetical acronym that may break span handling.\n  - The trigger covers all five. The \"finding vs. disease\" ambiguity in Ex 3 is secondary.\n  - \"Coats telangiectasis\" (TP) shares the head noun \"telangiectasis\" but is correctly tagged because the eponym \"Coats\" supplies the anchor. This confirms the anchor, not the head noun, is the discriminating feature.\n\n**Step 2: Pattern Formulation**\n\n- **Pattern Insight**\n  - **Pattern Name:** Descriptive or enzymatic disease names lacking a nomenclature anchor\n  - **Confusion Trigger:** A disease denoted by a compositional phrase whose head is a generic pathology or defect noun (\"telangiectasis,\" \"deficiency,\" \"deficient activity of\"), combined with an anatomical or gene/enzyme modifier (\"retinal,\" \"G6PD,\" \"in G6PD\"). The model treats the modifier as a non-disease entity (Anatomy/Gene) or the head as a clinical finding, so the span is labeled O. Parenthetical acronyms and \"so-called\" glosses further obscure the span.\n  - **Contrastive Evidence:** True positives such as \"Coats telangiectasis,\" \"Coats disease,\" \"Norrie disease,\" and \"SLS\" carry an eponym, a \"disease\" or \"syndrome\" head, or an acronym, which anchors the span as a named disorder. The same head noun (\"telangiectasis\") is correctly labeled when the eponym is present, which isolates the anchor as the discriminating feature.\n  - **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** Label a span SpecificDisease when a noun phrase names a heritable or pathological condition through a functional or defect head (\"deficiency,\" \"telangiectasis,\" \"deficient activity of X\"), and either:\n    1. the phrase is introduced or restated as a term by a metalinguistic marker (\"so-called,\" parentheses, or apposition to an already-named disease), or\n    2. the construction is \"deficiency (in/of) ⟨gene or enzyme⟩,\" where the gene or enzyme is a constituent of the disease name.\n\n    Gene, enzyme, and anatomical tokens inside such a span must not force an O label or be split off as separate entities. Include any internal parenthetical acronym (e.g., \"G6PD\") in the span.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4351, "output_tokens": 6649, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 5346}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}